.08611622,0.030946,0.02522543,0.160638,0.00639287,0.06792706,0.00725391,-0.08980461,0.01970062,0.00778066,0.00192012,0.07633318,0.07492148,0.18505582,-0.05907399,-0.0092531,0.11616392,0.00395615,0.02147566,-0.02170613,-0.05198282,0.04190582,-0.01790877,-0.02598498,0.02356174,0.02116003,-0.01180919,0.011518,-0.03698317,0.0481054,0.05906077,-0.07184082,0.04058234,-0.03063003,0.01863433,0.05017176,0.01154537,-0.00901099,-0.0138455,-0.04216508,0.03198043,0.02413049,-0.11791222,-0.06622345,0.02592372,-0.02242882],"last_embed":{"hash":"dsagnb","tokens":370}}},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"dsagnb","at":1771183427906},"key":"32LandformsRemoteSensing.md#3.2 Landforms and Remote Sensing#Types of Landforms","lines":[39,176],"size":15098,"outlinks":[{"title":"River Systems and Fluvial Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/fluvial-landforms.htm","line":12},{"title":"River Systems and Fluvial Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/fluvial-landforms.htm","line":25},{"title":"Chaco Canyon Map Link","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-12020455.6682%2C4307876.2919%2C-12016292.2388%2C4310004.5854%2C102100","line":28},{"title":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-12020455.6682%2C4307876.2919%2C-12016292.2388%2C4310004.5854%2C102100","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/?z=15&y=36.06092498632109&x=-107.96516464019793&basemap=null&layerIds=\"></iframe>\n\nA meandering stream has a single channel that winds like a snake through its valley, causing the distance “as the stream flows” to be greater than “as the crow flies.” As water flows around these curves, the water on the outer edge moves faster than on the inner edge. This difference in flow creates an erosional surface on the outer edge, known as a cut bank, and a depositional surface on the inner edge, called a point bar. When the bends of two meanders meet, they can bypass the river’s curve, forming an oxbow lake, which may eventually fill with sediment.\n\nMeanders change position by eroding sideways and slightly downstream. The sideways movement occurs because the maximum velocity of the stream shifts toward the outside of the bend, leading to erosion of the outer bank. Simultaneously, the reduced current inside the meander causes sediment, mainly sand, to be deposited. As a result, the stream moves laterally without altering the size of its channel. Due to the slope of the channel, erosion is more effective on the downstream side of a meander. Consequently, in addition to growing laterally, the bends gradually migrate down the valley.\n\n[River Systems and Fluvial Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service","line":31},{"title":"Kanuti National Wildlife Refuge Map Link","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-16881051.7009%2C10037689.0861%2C-16847744.2659%2C10054715.4341%2C102100","line":39},{"title":"Aeolian (Dunes) Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/aeolian-landforms.htm","line":54},{"title":"Aeolian (Dunes) Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/aeolian-landforms.htm","line":61},{"title":"Glaciers and Glacial Landforms - Geology (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/glacial-landforms.htm","line":77},{"title":"Glacier National Park Map Link","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-12685008.7075%2C6209242.7679%2C-12618393.8374%2C6243295.464%2C102100","line":80},{"title":"Karst Landscapes - Caves and Karst (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/karst-landscapes.htm","line":97},{"title":"Karst Landscapes - Caves and Karst (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/karst-landscapes.htm","line":104},{"title":"Central Florida Karst Landscape Map Link","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-9158992.2259%2C3283946.8446%2C-9012233.1316%2C3352052.2368%2C102100","line":107},{"title":"Sandy Coast Landforms (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/sandy-coast-landforms.htm","line":120},{"title":"Santee River delta","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-8867960.0676%2C3898373.2463%2C-8794580.5205%2C3932425.9424%2C102100","line":122},{"title":"Rocky Coast Landforms (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/rocky-coast-landforms.htm","line":126},{"title":"Point Reyes National Seashore Map Link","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-13693946.305%2C4577451.8807%2C-13689360.0833%2C4579580.1742%2C102100","line":128},{"title":"Delta Landforms (U.S. National Park Service)","target":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/delta-landforms.htm?utm_source=article&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=experience_more&utm_content=small","line":132},{"title":"Mississippi River delta (bird's foot pattern - river-dominated)","target":"https://apps.nationalmap.gov/viewer/viewer/index.html?extent=-10108118.627%2C3358485.9945%2C-9814600.4384%2C3494696.7789%2C102100","line":134}],"class_name":"SmartBlock","last_embed":{"hash":"dsagnb","at":1771183427906}},
